# 📝 Day 66 Reflection

Today I strengthened my Windows networking troubleshooting skills by learning how to use commands such as `ipconfig`, `ping`, `nslookup`, `tracert`, and `pathping` to isolate connectivity and DNS problems.

I also reviewed DHCP troubleshooting and learned that a 169.254.x.x APIPA address usually indicates that a Windows client failed to obtain an IPv4 address from DHCP. Instead of automatically blaming the DHCP server, I learned to investigate the client, network connection, VLAN, and DHCP environment.

Another important lesson was understanding the relationship between DNS and Active Directory. DNS allows domain clients to locate domain controllers and other AD-related services.

The biggest lesson from today's Help Desk practice was to **use evidence to guide troubleshooting**. I should not automatically run every command or make assumptions about the cause. I should gather information, test systematically, interpret the results, make authorized changes, verify the solution, and document the ticket.
